    "findings": [
      "The transaction count endpoint applies a cached response decorator and obtains its count from a TransactionSearch index query.",
      "The transaction download endpoint creates or reuses a separate export job. Export-job reuse depends on its stored request and data-load/submission-window dates, separately from count-response caching.",
      "The export helper retrieves transaction IDs using a search point in time, writes a job lookup and joins that lookup to database transaction rows. It obtains its own count rather than carrying the earlier public count request's membership.",
      "The contract export adds an is_fpds=True database filter before writing CSV rows through a SQL export. An index count is therefore not an atomic proof of the final CSV row count.",
      "The ID-collection loop contains a five-minute timeout branch that warns and returns collected IDs. No runtime evidence establishes that this branch, cache reuse, a missing database join or source updates caused the observed discrepancies."
    ],
    "scope": "Read-only source review of the specified ranges. No upstream code executed, public service modified, production version certified or historical job/cache/database state recovered."
